#!/bin/sh
# Flow offloading: ON by default again (2026-10-27, second change of the day).
#
# History, so nobody flips this back by accident:
#   v1.3 shipped flow_offloading=1 / flow_offloading_hw=1. Handing the nft
#   flowtable to the PPE broke IPv6 TCP on this SoC (handshake in ~1 ms, then
#   zero bytes ever); IPv4 was fine. So the defaults were flipped to 0 / 0 the
#   same day, which cost the wired IPv4 acceleration (>=395 Mbit/s with the PPE
#   vs the ~305/190 Mbit/s software ceiling).
#
# The cause was not "IPv6 in the PPE" but *where frames go* once the PPE is
# active: zx_ppe_start() moves the ports' MACs from the trap table into the ONU
# "routed" table, and the classifier had no IPv6 rule - so an IPv6 frame
# addressed to the router had no path left to the CPU. The IPv6 ethertype trap
# is now never lifted (zx_update_traps() in zx279128s-ppe.c keeps ETH_P_IPV6
# armed in every state), which puts IPv6 back on the CPU path while IPv4 keeps
# the hardware path. So 1 / 1 is safe again, and that is what this image ships.
#
# Still true, with hw offload on:
#  - IPv6 is not accelerated. It is switched in hardware but the L3 forwarding
#    stays on the CPU (see the note above). Wired IPv4 TCP/UDP is offloaded.
#  - WiFi-originated flows are rejected by the PPE on purpose (ingress must be
#    a switch port) - they stay on the CPU and top out around 200 Mbit/s.
#  - PPPoE uplinks may not take the hardware path (sibling ZX279133 behaviour).
#  - the hash table holds 384 entries; overflow falls back to software
#    (counters ppe_add_skip / ppe_add_fail under
#    /sys/kernel/debug/92000000.ethernet/)
#  - the classifier's IPv6 tables do exist in the driver (zx279128s-ppe.c,
#    extract index 10 + rule 0x99) but are off unless the module parameter
#    ipv6=1 is set: on hardware they could wedge the switch, and the vendor's
#    own IPv6 table templates are incomplete (docs/known-issues.md,
#    PPE-IPV6-TCP). Do not enable them expecting working IPv6.
#
# Turn them off from LuCI (Network -> Firewall -> General Settings) or:
#   uci set firewall.@defaults[0].flow_offloading_hw='0'
#   uci commit firewall && fw4 restart
uci -q batch <<-EOF
	set firewall.@defaults[0].flow_offloading='1'
	set firewall.@defaults[0].flow_offloading_hw='1'
	commit firewall
EOF
exit 0
